#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_BIP.rst:463
msgid "Submerged Outlet Manifold"
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_BIP.rst:465
msgid "The submerged outlet manifold, sometimes called a launder, collects clarified water from the top of the clarifier. It is a horizontal pipe that extends along the length of the clarifier bay and is located above the plate settlers but below the surface of the water. The submerged pipe has orifices drilled into its top; water enters the pipe through the orifices and the pipe leads out of the clarifier bay."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_BIP.rst:467
msgid "The outlet manifold is designed to generate 5 cm of head loss to ensure uniform flow distribution between clarifier bays and to have the majority of the head loss through the orifices to obtain uniform flow distribution between the orifices."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_BIP.rst:470
msgid "Outlet Weir"
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_BIP.rst:472
msgid "The submerged outlet manifold transports water from the clarifier bay to a collector channel that runs perpendicular to the clarifier bays (see :numref:`figure_spec_diffuser_dimensions`). All of the clarifier bay outlet manifolds deliver the clarified water to the collector channel. Water leaves the collector channel by flowing over the outlet weir. The elevation of the outlet weir controls the water levels in the clarifier and in the flocculator. The outlet weir does not need to be adjustable and small elevation errors are accommodated because water can flow in the collector channel with less head loss than the orifice head loss."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_BIP.rst:474
msgid "The outlet weir makes it possible to refill and empty individual clarifier bays with clarified water to ensure that after returning a clarifier bay to service the first water is of high quality."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_BIP.rst:477
msgid "Outlet Channel"
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_BIP.rst:479
msgid "After the water flows over the outlet weir, it is collected in the outlet channel. The water can be transported from the clarifier outlet channel to the filter inlet channel by pipes or by a channel."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_BIP.rst:482
msgid "Stacked Rapid Sand Filter |OStaRS|"
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_BIP.rst:0
msgid "OStaRS"
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_BIP.rst:484
msgid "For more detailed information see :ref:`Filtration Design <title_Filtration_Design>`. Stacked Rapid Sand, StaRS, filters (see :numref:`figure_spec_OStaRSoverview`) were invented in 2010 by the AguaClara Cornell program in response to the need for a new technology that would both eliminate the need for backwash pumps and not require the construction of 6 filters for small towns. As shown in :numref:`figure_spec_OStaRSfilterMode`, StaRS filters use six 20 cm deep layers of sand with the layers stacked vertically. The six layers give a total active sand depth of 1.2 m."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_BIP.rst:493
msgid "The open stacked rapid sand filters include advanced hydraulic controls to ensure stable operation during both filtration and backwash modes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_BIP.rst:496
msgid "Operation"
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_BIP.rst:498
msgid "The filter operates with the same design flow rate for both filtration (see :numref:`figure_spec_OStaRSfilterMode`) and backwash (see :numref:`figure_spec_OStaRSbackwashMode`) modes and uses clarified water for backwash. This eliminates the need for backwash pumps and ensures that the filters can be backwashed as long as clarified water is available."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_BIP.rst:507
msgid "OStaRS filtration mode"
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_BIP.rst:507
msgid "In filtration mode the flow divides between the six sand layers. The six sand layers operate in parallel during filtration."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_BIP.rst:517
msgid "OStaRS backwash mode"
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_BIP.rst:517
msgid "In backwash mode all of the flow enters at the bottom of the filter box and flows up through the six sand layers. The six sand layers operate in series during backwash."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_BIP.rst:523
msgid "Stacked Rapid Sand (StaRS) filters were developed to eliminate the need for backwash pumps and minimize the plan area required."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_BIP.rst:525
msgid "The filters should be designed so that the process of emptying the sand from the filter, removing the modules, cleaning the modules, replacing the modules, and replacing the sand is as easy as possible."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_BIP.rst:527
msgid "During backwash, all outlets and all inlets besides the bottom most inlet must be hydraulically isolated so all flow enters through the bottom inlet and flows out through the backwash siphon pipe."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_BIP.rst:529
msgid "The plant shall have a minimum of two StaRS filters so that one of the StaRS filters can be in operation while the other is offline for maintenance or repairs."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_BIP.rst:532
msgid "Configurations"
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_BIP.rst:534
msgid "Open StaRS (OStaRS) with 6 filter layers - used for flow rates greater than about 20 L/s. The minimum OStaRS flow rate is set by the minimum dimensions of the filter box that can be constructed and that enable filter maintenance."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_BIP.rst:536
msgid "Open StaRS (OStaRS) with 2 filter layers - used for flow between about 3 and 12 L/s. This technology is under development."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_BIP.rst:538
msgid "Enclosed StaRS (EStaRS) filters - used for lower flow rates. This technology is under development"
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_BIP.rst:540
msgid "Can be located on the same slab as the clarifier and flocculator because the EStaRS is operated under vacuum in backwash mode to achieve the necessary head for fluidizing the sand."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_BIP.rst:541
msgid "Assembled using PVC pipe as the body of the filter"
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_BIP.rst:542
msgid "Inner plumbing accessed through openings in the top and bottom of the main filter body"
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_BIP.rst:545
msgid "Sand Specification"
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_BIP.rst:547
msgid "StaRS filters use (6) six 20 cm deep layers of silica sand (no dual-media required) with the layers stacked vertically. The six layers give an active sand depth of 1.2 m. The grain size is 0.45 to 0.55 mm."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_BIP.rst:550
msgid "Filter Modules"
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_BIP.rst:552
msgid "The sand layers are contiguous and the only distinction between layers is the direction of flow during filtration. Each layer of sand sits in between an inlet and outlet filter module. Each module consists of a large diameter trunk inlet/outlet pipe, which connects to a row of smaller branch pipes. The branch pipes are supported along the filter walls by receptor pipes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_BIP.rst:554
msgid "The filter modules are anchored to the concrete slab to prevent uplift at the transition to backwash. The uplift forces are considerable and are detailed in :ref:`Backwash Initiation Forces <heading_StaRS_Backwash_Forces>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_BIP.rst:556
msgid "Inlet Filter Module"
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_BIP.rst:558
msgid "Small holes (orifices) are drilled into the inlet branches (see :numref:`figure_spec_OStaRSinletBranches`). The orifice diameter is selected based on constructability and not being too small to risk clogging (between 4 and 10 mm)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_BIP.rst:560
msgid "During filtration mode, water flows into the inlet filter modules through the inlet trunk and into the branches. Water flows out of the branches through small holes, into the space created by the wing, and then into the sand."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_BIP.rst:562
msgid "During the transition from the backwash to filtration modes, a small volume of water flows back into the inlet pipes. The “wings,” PVC pipes cut longitudinally, are affixed to the inlet branches to prevent sand from flowing into the inlet pipe. Wings are only included on the inlet filter modules."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_BIP.rst:564
msgid "Outlet Filter Module"
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_BIP.rst:566
msgid "The slots in the outlet branches (see :numref:`figure_spec_OStaRSoutletBranches`) are small enough to prevent sand from passing through. The filter modules shall be adequately supported to limit deflection of any of the module pipes to 2 millimeters or less to prevent significant opening or closing of the slots."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_BIP.rst:568
msgid "During filtration mode, water flows from the filter media into the slots and then through the branches and into the trunk pipes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_BIP.rst:570
msgid "During backwash mode, the outlet trunks are closed or isolated and water does not flow through the outlet modules."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_BIP.rst:579
msgid "OStaRS internal piping"
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_BIP.rst:579
msgid "The seven filter modules stack inside the filter box. Each module is held together using band clamps. The only pipe connections that are glued in the filter modules are the end caps on the trunks and receptors."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_BIP.rst:588
msgid "OStaRS inlet branch"
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_BIP.rst:588
msgid "The inlet branches use orifices to obtain uniform flow distribution and a wing system to prevent sand from entering into the branches during backwash and filter mode transitions."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_BIP.rst:597
msgid "OStaRS outlet branch"
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_BIP.rst:597
msgid "The outlet branches use 0.2 mm slots to keep the sand in the filter bed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_BIP.rst:601
msgid "Backwash Siphon"
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_BIP.rst:603
msgid "The siphon should be designed so the airlock can hold the water in the filter box at least until the filter reaches the design maximum head loss. Backwash is initiated when the operator briefly opens a small diameter air release valve to remove the trapped air from the siphon pipe. The siphon diameter must be sufficient to lower the water in the filter at an average velocity that is equal to or exceeds the backwash velocity."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_BIP.rst:606
msgid "Backwash Flow Control Weirs"
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_BIP.rst:608
msgid "The backwash gate (see :numref:`figure_spec_OStaRShydraulicControls`) ensures there is adequate flow to backwash one filter as long as there is at least that much flow entering the plant. This is important because many water treatment plants operate under reduced flow during the dry season."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_BIP.rst:610
msgid "Removing the backwash gate in front of the desired backwash filter will create the desired backwash flow rate for the filter, while evenly distributing the remaining flow rate to the other filters. By removing the backwash gate the flow control for that filter is shifted from the wide weir to the slot weir."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_BIP.rst:612
msgid "The slot weir sets the backwash flow rate and ensures that the backwash flow doesn't cause sand loss by expanding the sand bed excessively. The slot weir can be partially filled at plant commissioning to reduce the maximum backwash flow if needed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_BIP.rst:621
msgid "OStaRS hydraulic controls"
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_BIP.rst:621
msgid "The hydraulic controls give the operator full control of filter operation while ensuring that the flow is distributed evenly between filters in filtration mode and that a filter in backwash mode receives the correct backwash flow rate."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_BIP.rst:625
msgid "Sand Dump"
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_BIP.rst:627
msgid "The sand dump is critical for StaRS filters because the filter bed piping would make manual removal very difficult."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_BIP.rst:629
msgid "A sand dump pipe shall be installed in the filter box to allow for the filter media to be removed when the filter is in backwash mode and the sand bed is fluidized."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_BIP.rst:631
msgid "The sand dump pipe must be designed so that the sand doesn’t collect at one location in the pipe and cause a clog when the flow of the sand slurry is stopped. This requirement is met by using a single straight pipe with a slope less than the angle of repose of sand in water."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_BIP.rst:633
msgid "The sand dump velocity shall be at least 3 m/s to prevent the sand from settling in the sand dump pipe during operation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_BIP.rst:635
msgid "The sand dump is activated by removing a clamp on connector and cap assembly from the end of the pipe."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_BIP.rst:646
msgid "OStaRS sand dump"
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_BIP.rst:646
msgid "The sand dump is straight to prevent sand blockages and to minimize head loss."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_BIP.rst:649
msgid "Backwash Recycle"
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_BIP.rst:651
msgid "Backwash recycle is recommended for all communities where low flow conditions are likely to require water rationing. The backwash water storage tank is designed to hold the flow from one backwash event. A centrifugal pump can then be used to meter the water back to the entrance tank at a flow rate that empties the backwash water storage tank before the next filter needs to be backwashed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_BIP.rst:653
msgid "In the event of a power failure the backwash recycle system will instead use backwash to waste to dispose of the spent backwash filter water."
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_BIP.rst:656
msgid "Disinfection"
msgstr ""

#: ../../AIDE/Specifications_BIP.rst:658
msgid "Sodium or calcium hypochlorite is metered into the filtered water as it exits the filter hydraulic controls and enters the pipe that carries the water to a chlorine contact tank or directly to the community distribution tank. The chlorine is added after the previous treatment processes to reduce the production of disinfection byproducts and to enable biofiltration. The chlorine concentration is set by adjusting the position of the slider on the doser (see :numref:`figure_spec_doser`)."
msgstr ""
